.Im MicroEmacs command|buffer|macrodevelop
.ds C0 "(\fB\*(cX=\fR)\ \&
.so me.tni
.TH buffer-info 2
.XI buffer-info - "\*(C0Status information on current buffer position"
.SH NAME
buffer-info \- Status information on current buffer position
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.I n
\fBbuffer-info\fR \*(C0
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B buffer-info
reports on the current and total lines and  characters of the current  buffer.
It also  gives  the  hexadecimal  code of the  character  currently  under the
cursor.
.PP
The output of the command is displayed on the message line e.g.
.CS
Line 1845/3955 Col 0/0 Char 78267/167172 (46%) Win Line 99/48 ACol 0/0 char 10 (0x0a)
.CE 0
.PP
At the end of a line the line's own line ending is given, e.g. "\fCchar 13, 10 (0D, 0A - CRLF)\fR",
"\fCchar 10 (0A - LF)\fR" or "\fCno char (no line end)\fR", and "\fCno char (end of buffer)\fR" at the end of the
buffer. In a version of \*(mS without Unicode support a character from 0x80 is also given with its Unicode value
in the buffer's code page (see
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 ),
e.g. "\fCchar 228 (0xe4 U+0434)\fR" in a CP-1251 file, when the code page has one.
.PP
In a version of \*(mS with Unicode support (see
.Ht &opt 4 ,
label \fCuni\fR) the column and character counts are in characters and a byte count is added, and the character
under the cursor is given with its Unicode code point, e.g.:
.CS
Line 1/3 Col 3/6 Char 3/12 Byte 3/13 (23%) Win Line 0/21 ACol 3/6 char 233 (U+00E9)
.CE 0
.PP
In a file read with a code page (see
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 )
the byte is given first, e.g. "\fCchar 233 (E9, U+00E9)\fR", and a character the code page does not have is given as
"\fCchar U+20AC (not in iso8859-1)\fR". A raw byte (a byte which is not valid in the file's encoding) is given as
"\fCraw byte (0xFF)\fR".
.PP
.Ht $result 5
is set to the same output  string  unless a numeric  value of zero is given to
the command in which case the values are given in a
.Hl "\*(mS list" &lset 4
format and not printed to the message line; this  information can be useful in
macros. The column, line length and character counts of the list are in bytes, a version of \*(mS with Unicode
support appends 4 more values: the column, the line length, the characters before the cursor and the total characters,
all counted in characters.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
.Ht $result 5 ,
